@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import {
    delegation, brief text, queue place, queued task,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  parent adapter, supported host, entry point,
}
@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task, task id, child run, result, domain event, transcript, enqueue time,
    start time, child driver,
}
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, project root, unknown agent error, load error,
  unresolved root error, model endpoint, model tier, routable tier,
      tier error, agent entry, agents map, general agent, agent file, parallel limit, SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR,
  fallback agent,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  parent session id, event log, task status,
}
@design/routing-hooks.sigil from RoutingHooks import {
  Agent tool, CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR,
}

component McpServer {
  goal {
    Let a Claude Code session or subagent make a delegation through the plugin's
    MCP server, with progress while it waits, a way to collect it later, a way
    to cancel it, and an honest report of whether its child has started.
  }

  state {
    The server knows the parent session id of the current conversation. The
    *SessionStart hook* of the plugin records it, so that listing can be limited to
    this conversation even after /clear. When the server does not yet know it, a
    delegation carries none.

    A *progress notification* carries a counter that rises with each notification
    and one *message line*, and no total. The message line names the agent, the
    kind
    of the latest domain event, the tool name with its target path for tool
    activity, and the elapsed time. While the child run is Pending it says that the
    run is waiting, and once the child run is released it no longer says so.

    McpServer is a parent adapter for Claude Code sessions: the entry point of its
    process registers it with Kernel as a parent adapter, and it reaches the core
    only through DelegationService, for the delegation, the collect, the cancel and
    the listing, and through AgentConfig, and through Kernel only to list the
    supported hosts that it gives AgentConfig, and reports each queued task that
    DelegationService derives,
    never as started.

    A *status line* reports a task that has no result yet. For a queued task of
    this process it gives the model endpoint, the queue place and the time
    waited since the enqueue time, and for a queued task of another process it
    gives the model endpoint and the time waited with no queue place:
    ```text
    queued, not launched yet: no child is running; it starts when endpoint <e> has room (place <n> in this session's queue, waiting <elapsed>)
    ```
    For a task whose child run has a start time the status line gives that
    start time and how long ago it was:
    ```text
    running, started <iso> (<elapsed> ago)
    ```

    The *plugin data folder* is the folder that Claude Code gives the plugin for
    its own files. It holds the recorded parent session id of each project.
  }

  logic {
    When the server resolves the project root, it writes a *readiness marker*
    named by its own process id in the folder of that project under the
    plugin data folder, holding its process id and the process id of its
    parent, the Claude Code process that started it. On exit it removes the
    readiness marker, which ends that flow.

    On each tool listing, read the agent file again, giving AgentConfig each
    supported host that Kernel lists, and build the description of
    the delegate tool with each routable tier and the host and model it runs, which
    ends that flow. When the load fails, the description falls back to the
    static text, and the tools are still listed.

    On a delegate call that waits, make a delegation with the parent session id of
    the caller. When the request carries a progress token, send a
    progress notification to the caller for each domain event, at most once a
    second with the latest message line, and after every 30 seconds of silence,
    including while the child run is Pending. A silent heartbeat repeats the latest
    message line with the new elapsed time. Sending the progress notification ends
    that branch. Return the result when the task settles, which ends the flow.

    On a delegate call that does not wait, return the task id at once with its
    status line, which ends that flow. A later await call returns the result of
    each task that has settled, or the status line of a task that has not when
    the await timeout ends, and the flow ends there.

    When Claude Code cancels a waited call with notifications/cancelled, cancel its
    task, which ends the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    The server must expose four tools, the delegate tool, the *tasks tool*, the
    await tool and the *cancel tool*, under
    names of the form mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__ followed by the tool, and only
    the delegate tool, which starts a delegation, and the await tool must be
    always loaded, and no other tool may be.

    The *server instructions* must carry only the delegation rules. They must tell
    the parent to write a self-contained brief text, because the child cannot see
    the conversation, and they must not say that a delegation needs a configured
    agent definition, because the general agent runs work that names none.

    Every tool must return a bounded result and none may return a transcript.

    An await reply must give each collected task an equal share of the
    *tool result limit*, and a section cut to its share must end by naming
    the tasks tool with that task id as the way to read the whole result.

    No reply of the delegate tool, the tasks tool or the await tool may call a
    queued task started or
    running, and every reply about a task whose child run has a start time and
    no result must give its status line with that start time.

    A progress notification must not carry agent message text, tool output, tool
    arguments other than a path, the stderr tail or skill content of the child run,
    because the event log must never reach the parent.

    The server must pass the roots that Claude Code reports to the delegation
    service and must never use the working directory of its own process as the
    project root. The plugin sets SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR from CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR for
    it.

    No agent name or the agent name general, when no general agent is configured,
    and a name other than general that the agents map does not hold, when no
    fallback agent is configured, must each return the unknown agent error as a
    *tool error* that names every configured agent entry. When a
    fallback agent is configured, a delegation that names a name other than general
    that the agents map does not hold runs the fallback agent, and the reply must
    say that
    the named agent is not configured. A load error of the agent file
    must be returned as a tool error as well, with no task made.

    An error of the driver registry for a host with no registered child driver is
    returned like a tier error, and a tier error must be returned as a tool error
    that carries the tier error unchanged, with the tier it names, every configured
    model tier and, when the
    host is the cause, that host and every supported host, and says to dispatch
    that subagent through the Agent tool
    instead, as a pass-back whose short description begins with passback, with no
    task made.

    The unresolved root error must be returned as a tool error that names
    SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR and says that the configuration must be fixed by the
    user, so that a calling subagent does not retry the call.

    When the server does not know its parent session id, the tasks tool with no
    argument must list every task and say that the conversation could not be
    identified.

    A listing from the tasks tool must hold at most 50 tasks, the tasks with a
    start time and no result first, then each queued task, then the rest newest
    first, and it must end with a notice that names how many tasks it left out
    and says that a task can still be read by its task id. This cap is fixed
    and applies whether or not the conversation is known.

    Closing the stdin of the server while a child run is live must leave no process
    of that child run behind.

    The server must write its readiness marker only after its project root
    resolves and must remove it on exit, and a readiness marker whose process is
    no longer alive must count as absent.

    A plugin cannot ship an *allow rule*. McpServer assumes that the user or
    project supplies an allow rule for mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__* before a
    delegation from a subagent. Without it, the delegation raises an approval
    prompt in the main session, and under dontAsk it is denied and the subagent
    sees the denial.

    McpServer depends on DelegationService for the delegation and the
    queue place, and on AgentConfig for the unknown agent error, the tier error
    and the routable tier names, and on Kernel to list the supported hosts, and
    on RoutingHooks for what the SessionStart hook adds to Claude's context after
    it records the parent session id, and it must not depend on any particular
    child driver.
  }

  cases {
    In every case here the agent file also holds the default tier and a general
    agent.

    A delegate call with wait false returns its task id at once, and await with a
    short timeout returns no result for that task while its child run is still
    live, only its status line.

    Three delegate calls with wait false on a model endpoint whose
    parallel limit is 1 return the first as running with its start time and
    the second and third as a queued task at place 1 and 2. The tasks tool on
    the second returns its queued status line and never says that its child run
    is running or has started, and after
    the first settles, await on the second returns it running with a
    start time later than its enqueue time.

    A queued task of another process is listed as queued with its time waited
    counted from its enqueue time and no queue place.

    Listing with no argument returns only each task recorded for the current
    parent session id, and returns every task with a notice when the server does
    not know it.

    A listing of 60 tasks returns 50, with every task that has a start time and
    no result among them, and a notice that 10 tasks were left out and that
    each can be read by its task id.

    A delegate call with an unknown agent name and no model tier, when no fallback
    agent is configured, returns a tool error that names every configured
    agent entry and makes no task. With a fallback agent configured, the same call
    runs the fallback agent and its reply says that the named agent is not
    configured.

    A delegate call with a model tier that is not configured returns a
    tool error that lists every configured model tier and names the Agent tool,
    and makes no task.

    A tool listing with the model tier medium on r9700/Qwen3.8 shows medium and
    r9700/Qwen3.8 in the delegate description. After the agent file changes, the
    next listing shows the new routable tier set, and with a broken agent file
    the tools are still listed with the static description.

    An await over six settled tasks whose results together exceed the
    tool result limit returns a section for every task, and each cut section
    ends with the pointer to tasks with its task id.
  }

  interface {
    Let the *delegate tool*, mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__delegate, accept a
    call with an optional agent name, an optional model tier written as medium
    or tier:medium, a brief text, optional files, an optional expected output
    and an optional wait that defaults to true. Return the result with the
    task id first.

    Record the parent session id of each conversation through the
    SessionStart hook at each session start.

    Describe the delegate tool with each routable tier and the host and model it
    runs,
    read again from the agent file at each tool listing.

    Let the tasks tool list every task of the current parent session id, or return
    one task by its task id. Bound a listing to 50 tasks and say how many it left
    out.

    Let the *await tool* collect the result of tasks started without waiting, and
    let
    the cancel tool end a task as cancelled.

    Report each task that has no result by its status line in the replies of
    delegate, tasks and await, so that a queued task is never reported as
    started.

    Keep a readiness marker in the plugin data folder for each project while the
    server runs, holding its process id and the process id of its parent.

    Be registered with Kernel as a parent adapter for Claude Code sessions by the
    entry point of its process, which is outside McpServer and which also imports
    and registers the child drivers that process needs.

    Assume an allow rule from the user or project for a delegation from a subagent,
    because the plugin cannot ship one.

    Send a progress notification to a caller that asked for progress, at least once
    every 30 seconds while a delegation is running.
  }

  decisions {
    Only delegate and await are always loaded, so that a parent can dispatch a
    batch and collect it with no tool search hop, while the other tools stay out
    of the parent context. Progress
    carries one line from the latest event so that the parent sees real activity
    without receiving the event log. The allow rule stays an assumption in the
    spike because a plugin cannot ship one: the README and the allow-list of each
    delegating experiment arm supply it, and a setup command is left to a later
    version.

    A queued task is reported as not launched yet because compound-engineering
    starts each reviewer's 20-minute clock at its launch, and a reviewer late in
    the queue of a one-GPU model endpoint would otherwise time out before it
    ran. Queued is derived and not stored because the start-up sweep, the
    listing and the command line all key on the task status running. The
    status line says this session's queue because the queue place is counted
    per process. Each task in an await gets its own share of the
    tool result limit so that one long result never hides the findings of the
    others.
  }
}
